## The IPv6 leg this case had to build (ordinary setup, flagged at triage)

vlan1 already carried a link-local, so IPv6 was enabled on the box; **the transit VLAN had
nothing**. Baseline, before any configuration:

```
awplus#show ipv6 interface brief
Interface             IP-Address         Status          Protocol
eth0          fe80::c462:afff:fe1b:a060/64    preferred    admin up    running
lo            unassigned                      N/A          admin up    running
vlan1         fe80::e21a:eaff:fed4:7043/64    preferred    admin up    running
vlan10        unassigned                      N/A          admin up    running
vlan48        unassigned                      N/A          admin up    running
vlan60        unassigned                      N/A          admin up    running
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
   (nothing)
```

Applied:
```
awplus(config)#interface vlan10
awplus(config-if)#ipv6 enable
awplus(config-if)#ipv6 address fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34/64
awplus#show ipv6 interface brief
vlan10        fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34/64      preferred    admin up    running
              fe80::e21a:eaff:fed4:7043/64    preferred
```
The prefix is not invented: the testbox's `eth3` already holds `fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1/64`, and
`eth3` is cabled to `port1.0.10`, which is the access port for vlan10. So the two ends are on
one /64 with no routing in between.

**Reachability proved before any NTP measurement** (a leg is proved before a silence on it is
read):
```
tools/tb ping tb277 eth3 fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34 3
3 packets transmitted, 3 received, 0% packet loss, rtt min/avg/max/mdev = 0.410/0.543/0.800/0.182 ms
```

