### STEP 2 -- Configure the server, and read it over IPv6 field by field

```
awplus(config)#ntp master 7
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
ntp master 7
awplus#show ntp status
   ... Leap status : Normal ...        (populated; it was completely empty before -- T33399 Finding 1)
```

```
tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 30 t44298-s2-on.pcap udp port 123       (background)
tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34 -6 \
     --bind fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 --count 3 --gap 2 --timeout 3
```
```
  #1 sent 2026-10-21 04:26:59.292399Z  reply 48 B from fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34:123
      LI=0(no-warning) VN=4 mode=4(server) stratum=7 poll=4 precision=-25
      refid=127.127.1.1 (ascii '....')  root_delay=0.000000s root_dispersion=0.000000s
      reference=2026-10-21 04:26:38.055104Z
      origin   =2026-10-21 04:26:59.292335Z  origin_echo=yes
      receive  =2026-10-21 04:26:59.292503Z
      transmit =2026-10-21 04:26:59.292614Z
      offset=-0.000160 s  delay=0.000528 s
SUMMARY: sent=3 replies=3 origin_echo=3 no_reply=0
         reply stratum(s)=7 mode(s)=4
         offset min=-0.000160 max=-0.000101 mean=-0.000122 s
```

| field | measured | why it is right |
| --- | --- | --- |
| transport | **IPv6**, source and destination both `fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::/64` | this is the IPv6 server case, not IPv4 with a v6 address written on it |
| mode | **4 (server)** | it answered as a server |
| LI | **0** | claiming synchronisation |
| stratum | **7** | exactly the `ntp master 7` value |
| refid | **127.127.1.1** | the local-clock reference, as in the IPv4 case |
| origin | **echoes our transmit, 3 of 3** | the replies answer these requests |
| offset | **-122 microseconds** (mean of 3) | and that is a measurement in its own right -- see below |

🔬 **The -122 microseconds is a continuity check on the whole group.** In T33399 the same
instrument measured this DUT's clock at **-14.269 s**. T33400 then pointed the DUT at the box's
`ntpd` and `chronyd` stepped it by 14.836 s. The NTP client was **removed** at the end of
T33400, so by this case the DUT has been free-running for about five minutes -- and it is still
within **160 microseconds** of the box. The discipline NTP applied in T33400 outlived the
configuration that applied it, which is exactly what a disciplined clock should do and is
independent confirmation that T33400's correction was real.

**STEP 2 PASS.**

### STEP 3 -- ONE `ntp master` serves BOTH address families, measured in ONE capture

With the configuration untouched, the same server was queried over IPv4 seconds later. The
single capture `t44298-s2-on.pcap` holds both conversations:

```
04:26:59.292506 v6 fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1:43877   -> fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34:123 client
04:26:59.292888 v6 fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34:123    -> fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1:43877 server v4 LI=ok st=7 refid=127.127.1.1
04:27:01.296282 v6 ... client ;  04:27:01.296634 v6 ... server st=7
04:27:03.300130 v6 ... client ;  04:27:03.300497 v6 ... server st=7
04:27:03.864682 v4 10.38.22.33:45443 -> 10.38.22.34:123   client
04:27:03.865032 v4 10.38.22.34:123   -> 10.38.22.33:45443 server v4 LI=ok st=7 refid=127.127.1.1
04:27:05.868253 v4 ... client ;  04:27:05.868600 v4 ... server st=7

  10 frames in the capture, 10 NTP packets on port 123
  by family: IPv4=4  IPv6=6
  by mode:   client(3)=5  server(4)=5
  10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34                              2
  10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33                              2
  fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 -> fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34       3
  fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34 -> fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1       3
  pairing:   requests=5 replies=5  paired=5 unpaired=0 unanswered=0
```

**5 requests, 5 replies, 5 paired, 0 unanswered, across two address families, same stratum,
same refid.** There is no separate IPv6 enable for the NTP server and none is offered: a single
`ntp master` serves IPv4 and IPv6 at once.

**STEP 3 PASS.**

### STEP 4 -- `ntp restrict default-v6` discriminates BY ADDRESS FAMILY

The control that must not match, again run against a live server so that only one thing differs
-- here, the address family itself:

```
awplus(config)#ntp restrict default-v6 serve deny
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
ntp master 7
ntp restrict default-v6 serve deny
```

| leg | client | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **denied** | `fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1` (IPv6) | `sent=3 replies=0 origin_echo=0 no_reply=3`, ntpc **exit 2** |
| **allowed**, same server, seconds later | `10.38.22.33` (IPv4) | `sent=2 replies=2 origin_echo=2 no_reply=0`, stratum 7 |

A single command switched off time service for **every** IPv6 client while leaving IPv4
untouched. The denial is silent -- no kiss-o'-death, no ICMPv6 -- exactly as the IPv4 denial was
in T33399.

Removed, and IPv6 service returned on the same leg:
```
awplus(config)#no ntp restrict default-v6
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
ntp master 7
  -> ntpc -6 ...: SUMMARY sent=2 replies=2 origin_echo=2, stratum 7
```

Note the negation form, which is the same asymmetry T33399 recorded for the IPv4 address form:
the configured line reads `ntp restrict default-v6 serve deny`, but it is removed with
**`no ntp restrict default-v6`** -- the `serve deny` qualifier cannot be typed back.

**STEP 4 PASS.**

### STEP 5 -- Persistence across a cold boot, measured ON THE WIRE, over IPv6

```
awplus#write
Building configuration...
[OK]
```
The write added to startup-config exactly three things, and nothing else:
```
> ntp master 7
> !
>  ipv6 address fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34/64
>  ipv6 enable
```
plus the saved-on comment.

```
tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
17:28:41 >>> reload   (04:28:41 UTC)
reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
17:29:42 login seen after 61 s
```
**61 s cold boot** -- a fifth measurement in line with the other four (60, 60, 60, 61 s).

**Eleven seconds after the login prompt, with nothing typed into the DUT** -- no login, no
configuration, no `ipv6 address`:
```
tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 40 t44298-s6-persist.pcap udp port 123   (background)
tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34 -6 --bind fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 \
     --count 6 --gap 3 --timeout 3

  #1 sent 2026-10-21 04:29:54.408293Z  reply 48 B from fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34:123
      LI=0(no-warning) VN=4 mode=4(server) stratum=7 poll=4 precision=-25
      refid=127.127.1.1   origin_echo=yes
      offset=-0.179033 s  delay=0.002262 s
SUMMARY: sent=6 replies=5 origin_echo=5 no_reply=1
         reply stratum(s)=7 mode(s)=4
         offset min=-0.179137 max=-0.178943 mean=-0.179010 s
```
`ntpwire`:
```
  11 NTP packets; by family: IPv6=11 ; by mode: client(3)=6 server(4)=5
  pairing: requests=6 replies=5 paired=5 unpaired=0 unanswered=1
```

**Both halves of the saved configuration came back by themselves** -- the global IPv6 address on
vlan10 (otherwise there would have been nothing to answer) **and** `ntp master 7` (the replies
carry stratum 7) -- and the DUT was serving IPv6 time 11 s after the login prompt.

The one unanswered request of six is the **default NTP rate limiter** measured in T33399
(burst 8 / interval 8 s / leak 4, on by default and invisible in running-config), not a
persistence failure: the five that were answered are all stratum 7 with the origin echoed.

By-product: the clock drifted **179 ms** across this reboot, against 593 ms across T33400's and
about 570 ms across T33399's -- all sub-second, all consistent with a working RTC.

**STEP 5 PASS.**

### STEP 6 -- Removal: the IPv6 server stops, and the counters go dark

```
awplus(config)#no ntp master
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
   (nothing)
awplus#show ntp status
   (completely empty)

tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34 -6 --bind fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 \
     --count 3 --gap 1 --timeout 3
SUMMARY: sent=3 replies=0 origin_echo=0 no_reply=3          (exit 2)
```
The IPv6 address was still on vlan10 and still pinging at this point, so the silence is the
server being gone and not the leg.

**STEP 6 PASS.**

